        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:37:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62570,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Corpus luteum cyst most often occurs in women of reproductive age, although it may rarely be seen in neonates or after menopause. Most patients are asymptomatic, but corpus luteum cyst may present with irregular menstruation, abdominal pain, or haemoperitoneum due to rupture {6720774}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:37:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62571,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Corpus luteum cysts are common.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:37:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62572,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:37:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62573,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>They result from delay in involution and cystic degeneration of a large haemorrhagic corpus luteum.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:37:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62574,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The cut surface is yellow and convoluted, with a central cystic and haemorrhagic cavity.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:37:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62575,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Corpus luteum cysts have an undulating thick lining composed of large luteinized granulosa cells with uniform small nuclei and abundant large eosinophilic cytoplasm. An outer layer of smaller luteinized theca interna cells is also present. The differential diagnosis includes pregnancy luteoma, Leydig cell tumour, and steroid cell tumour NOS.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:37:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62576,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:37:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62577,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:37:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62578,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 117,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a large cyst (&gt;&nbsp;3&nbsp;cm); an undulating thick lining composed of large luteinized granulosa cells; exclusion of pregnancy luteoma, Leydig cell tumour, and steroid cell tumour NOS.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-14T11:37:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
